# Genome-wide CRISPR Screen for IFNG Regulation - Round Analysis

## 1. Reflection

The screen has identified 79 high-impact hits with strong effect sizes. Key observations: (1) Negative regulators of IFNG are emerging clearly (TBX21, ZAP70, VAV1, CD3D/E/G, LCP2, PLCG1 all show large negative scores ~-2 to -4), suggesting TCR signaling suppression; (2) Positive regulators include CBLB, MAP4K1, NFKB2, TSC1 (scores 1.0-1.6); (3) The moderate-scoring genes (±0.1-0.4) from Round 1 may be secondary regulators worth exploring further; (4) Functional clustering suggests immune signaling hubs (JAK-STAT, NF-κB, TCR) are critical. Strategy: Target unexplored genes in validated pathways and genes with similar functional domains/interaction networks to identified hits.

## 2. Research Plan

Based on the hit profile, I will prioritize: (1) Co-factors and upstream regulators of identified TCR/JAK-STAT/NF-κB hits (e.g., PTEN, SHB-family, LAG3, CD247, THEMIS2); (2) Chromatin remodelers and transcriptional regulators structurally similar to BRD4/BRD9 and HDAC family members; (3) Ubiquitin ligases and adaptor proteins in immune signaling (PELI family, SMURF2, TRAF family extensions); (4) IL-2/IL-12 pathway components and downstream effectors not yet tested; (5) Negative regulators predicted by the strong negative hit scores. This approach exploits pathway coherence observed in the hits while maintaining coverage across untested gene families critical to IFNG regulation.

