# 1. Reflection

The 79 hits identified show strong patterns: positive hits include genes involved in ubiquitination (UBE2F, UBE2E1, UBE2L3), chromatin remodeling (BRD4, BRD9), and T cell signaling amplifiers (TSC1, NFKB2, SOCS3). Negative hits are predominantly TCR/CD3 pathway components (CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, ZAP70, LCP2, VAV1) and JAK-STAT inhibitors (JAK1, JAK3, STAT3), suggesting IFNG production is suppressed by limiting early T cell activation signals. The tested genes show many negative regulators (IRF8, TET2, THEMIS, FOS) and positive regulators (SETDB1, DNMT1, NFKBIA, PAG1, USP7) at moderate effect sizes. To maximize discovery of high-impact genes, I should focus on: (1) genes in functionally similar pathways to strong positive hits, (2) genes controlling epigenetic/post-translational modifications, and (3) genes in metabolic pathways supporting Th1 differentiation.

# 2. Research Plan

I will prioritize genes in five complementary biological pathways: (1) E2F/cell cycle regulators and DNA replication factors (E2F3, E2F4, MCM genes) that may enhance IFNG expression similar to E2F1/E2F2; (2) additional ubiquitin-conjugating enzymes (UBE2D, UBE2L, UBE2Q, UBE2R, UBE2V2) and NEDD8 pathway components beyond those tested; (3) chromatin remodeling/histone modification enzymes (BAZ1A, BAZ1B, SRCAP, INO80B, SMARCD2) complementing BRD4/BRD9 hits; (4) NF-κB and MAPK pathway activators (NFKB1 analogs, MAP3K genes, TAB genes) that synergize with NFKB2's strong positive effect; and (5) metabolic regulators (AMPK/PRKAA genes, mTOR pathway components, glucose/lipid metabolism genes) supporting Th1 differentiation. This strategy leverages the strong positive signals from ubiquitination, NF-κB signaling, and chromatin opening while exploring mechanistically related pathways with maximal biological plausibility.

