# Analysis of CRISPR Screen Results

## 1. Reflection

The screen has identified 79 hits with strong effects on IFNG production. Key observations: (1) Negative hits include critical T cell signaling components (CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, ZAP70, LCP2, VAV1, PLCG1, LCK) and transcription factors (TBX21, JAK1), suggesting these are required for IFNG production; (2) Positive hits include epigenetic regulators (BRD4, BRD9, HDAC8, KDM1A) and immune checkpoints (CD28, CD27), suggesting their removal enhances IFNG; (3) The next round should prioritize genes in validated pathways: TCR/CD28 signaling, JAK-STAT pathway, chromatin remodeling, and metabolic regulators that directly interact with identified hits. I'll focus on untested genes that are functionally connected to the 79 hits.

## 2. Research Plan

Based on the strong signals from T cell receptor signaling (CD3 complex, ZAP70, LCP2) and JAK-STAT pathway (JAK1, JAK3, STAT3) hits, I will test genes encoding: (1) Additional TCR signaling components and adaptor proteins not yet tested; (2) Co-stimulatory and co-inhibitory pathway members; (3) Downstream STAT family members and their regulators; (4) Chromatin remodeling complexes related to identified HDAC and BRD family hits; (5) Metabolic checkpoint genes (mTOR pathway, glucose metabolism) that regulate T cell differentiation. This targeted approach maximizes discovery probability by building on validated biological pathways while maintaining pathway diversity.

