# Step 4 Analysis: CRISPR Screen for IFNG Regulators

## 1. Reflection

The screen has identified 79 high-impact hits with large effect sizes (|score| > 0.4), revealing key pathways regulating IFNG production. Strong negative regulators include CD3D (-3.31), ZAP70 (-3.92), VAV1 (-3.84), and CD3G (-3.63), indicating T cell receptor signaling suppresses basal IFNG. Strong positive regulators include MAP4K1 (+1.23), CBLB (+1.36), and NFKB2 (+1.58), suggesting kinase signaling and NF-κB activation promote IFNG. The next round should focus on: (1) genes in pathways containing hits showing extreme effect sizes, (2) known interactors of validated hits, (3) genes with similar functional annotations to top hits, and (4) remaining untested genes in critical IFNG-regulatory pathways (JAK-STAT, NF-κB, MAPK, SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling).

## 2. Research Plan

Based on pathway analysis of the 79 hits, I will prioritize testing untested genes in four categories: (1) JAK-STAT pathway components (JAK2 alternatives, STAT variants, and their negative regulators), (2) NF-κB signaling members (IKK complex, NEMO interactors, and RelA partners), (3) MAPK cascade elements (RAF family, MEK variants, ERK regulators), and (4) chromatin remodeling complexes (SWI/SNF members, histone deacetylases, and histone methyltransferases) since NFKB2, TSC1/2, BRD4, and BRD9 showed strong effects. Additionally, I will test co-stimulatory pathway components (CD40, ICAM1, CD80 homologs) and remaining ubiquitin/NEDD8 system members (given UBE2 family enrichment in hits). This strategy leverages the principle that functionally related genes often have correlated regulatory roles.

