# CRISPR Screen Analysis for IFNG Regulation

## 1. Reflection

The screen has identified 79 hits with strong phenotypes, revealing critical pathways in IFN-γ regulation. Key observations: (1) TCR/CD3 complex components (CD3D, CD3E, CD3G) show strong negative scores (~-3 to -3.6), suggesting TCR signaling suppresses IFNG; (2) Positive hits include MAP4K1 (+1.23), CBLB (+1.36), NFKB2 (+1.58), and TSC1 (+1.09), indicating mTOR and NF-κB pathway involvement; (3) Negative JAK/STAT hits (JAK1, JAK3, LCK, STAT3) suggest these promote basal IFNG but require specific contexts. Strategy: prioritize genes in proximal TCR signaling, mTOR regulation, metabolic sensing, and chromatin remodeling that haven't been tested, focusing on direct interactors and paralogs of validated hits.

## 2. Research Plan

I will prioritize untested genes based on: (1) direct physical interactors with strong hit proteins (e.g., SLP76, ITK, VAV family members); (2) parallel pathway components in mTOR/metabolic sensing (PRKAA2, AMPK family); (3) epigenetic regulators functionally related to positive hits (BRD family members, HDAC paralogs); (4) adaptor proteins and kinases in TCR proximal signaling not yet screened; (5) genes in interferon response amplification loops. This targeted approach should identify synergistic regulators more efficiently than random selection within the remaining 18,811 genes.

