# Genome-wide CRISPR Screen Analysis for IFNG Regulation

## 1. Reflection

The screen has identified 79 high-confidence hits with strong effects on IFNG production. Analysis reveals clear patterns: (1) TCR/CD3 signaling components (CD3D, CD3E, CD3G, ZAP70, LCP2) are strong negative regulators, suggesting T cell activation is critical; (2) JAK-STAT pathway members (JAK1, JAK3, STAT3) negatively regulate IFNG, indicating feedback suppression; (3) NF-κB pathway components (IKBKG, IKBKB, CHUK, RELA) show mixed effects; (4) positive regulators include NFKB2, TSC1/2, MAP4K1, and CBLB. The next round should prioritize: (a) unexplored components of validated pathways, (b) genes showing functional similarity to confirmed hits, and (c) upstream/downstream effectors of identified regulatory nodes.

## 2. Research Plan

Focusing on the most promising unscreened genes, I will prioritize: (1) core TCR signalosome members and adapters (LAT, SLP76, PAK1, GrpL1) that directly interact with ZAP70 and LCP2; (2) additional JAK-STAT family members (JAK2 is tested but STAT-interacting proteins like PTPN2/11 need validation) and phosphatases that regulate this axis; (3) chromatin remodeling complexes (CHD family members beyond CHD7, PBAF components) that may epigenetically control IFNG; (4) MAPK cascade components upstream of the identified MAPK1/3 hits (MAP2K1/2 tested, but RAF family and feedback regulators needed); and (5) metabolic regulators and mTOR/AMPK pathway members that intersect with TSC1/2 findings. This approach leverages the strong hits to guide systematic exploration of their biological networks.

