# Genome-Wide CRISPR Screen: Round N+1 Gene Selection

## 1. Reflection
The screen has identified 79 strong hits with substantial effect sizes (ranging from -3.92 to +1.58 log fold change). Key observations: (1) Negative regulators of IFNG include CD3D, ZAP70, VAV1, CD3G, LCP2, and PLCG1 (TCR signaling components), JAK1, and IFNGR1/IFNGR2; (2) Positive regulators include CBLB (E3 ubiquitin ligase), MAP4K1, NFKB2, PRKCH, and SMC3; (3) The moderate-scoring tested genes show weaker effects, suggesting the true regulators have been partially enriched. Strategy: prioritize untested genes predicted to functionally interact with identified hits and core IFNG regulatory pathways (JAK-STAT, TCR signaling, NF-κB, chromatin remodeling, protein ubiquitination).

## 2. Research Plan
Focus on untested genes in three categories: (1) Direct JAK-STAT pathway components and IFN signaling mediators missing from hits (STAT5 isoforms, JAK2 paralog functions, PIAS family members not yet saturated); (2) TCR proximal signaling and adapter proteins (LYN, FYN, SLP76, LAG3, TYK2-related kinases, and CD247); (3) Chromatin remodelers and transcriptional regulators (BAF complexes, additional KDM family members, HDAC isoforms, and AP-1 components) that regulate IFNG locus accessibility; (4) E3 ubiquitin ligases and deubiquitinases (SOCS family paralogs, TRIM/RBR family members not yet tested) that regulate signal transduction; (5) Understudied IL-12/IL-18 signaling nodes and metabolic checkpoints. This exploits the observation that tested genes cluster in specific pathways while maintaining diversity across functional modules to capture epistatic interactions.

